---
import Guide from '../layouts/Guide.astro'
import { doc, repo } from '../lib/content'
const ping = `POST /api/v1/usage/ping
{
  "v": 1,
  "product": "desktop",
  "version": "0.1.600",
  "os": "darwin",
  "arch": "arm64",
  "first": false,
  "newWeek": true,
  "newMonth": false
}`
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['summary', 'In short'],
  ['site', 'This website'],
  ['downloads', 'Download counts'],
  ['app', 'The app on your computer'],
  ['ping', 'The daily usage ping'],
  ['account', 'A Phosphor account'],
  ['services', 'Services we rely on'],
  ['delete', 'Deleting your data'],
  ['contact', 'Changes and questions'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Privacy"
  eyebrow="Policy / last updated 27 October 2026"
  description="Phosphor is an open-source app that runs on your computer. This page lists everything the project's own servers receive, how long it is kept, and how to remove it."
  sections={sections}
>
  <section id="summary">
    <h2>In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your prompts, code, files, transcripts and provider keys stay on your computer or your own
        machines. Phosphor's servers never receive them.
      </li><li>
        This website sets no cookies and runs no analytics or third-party scripts.
      </li><li>
        Download links add one to a daily total. Nothing about who clicked is stored.
      </li><li>
        Phosphor Desktop sends one anonymous ping a day so we can count active installs. It carries
        no ID, and you can turn it off.
      </li><li>
        A Phosphor account is optional. You only need one for remote access, and you can delete it
        yourself at any time.
      </li><li>
        Nothing is sold or shared for advertising. The source for every server is public in <a
          href={repo}>the repository</a
        >.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
  <section id="site">
    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      phosphor.saccolabs.com is a set of static pages. It has no cookies, no tracking pixels and no
      scripts from anyone else. The server's request log records the time, the route, the response
      status and how long it took. It doesn't record your IP address, and the log is kept only as
      long as the server's log rotation keeps it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="downloads">
    <h2>Download counts</h2>
    <p>
      The site's download links and install command go through <code>/download/…</code> on this
      server. It adds one to a total for that day and that link, then sends you to the GitHub
      release. The stored row is the date, which link, and the count. No address, cookie, referrer
      or browser details are stored with it.
    </p>
    <p>
      To stop one visitor inflating the totals, the server keeps a keyed hash of the address in
      memory for up to an hour. The key is random, made fresh every time the server starts, and
      never written down, so the hash can't be turned back into an address. Linking to GitHub
      directly skips the count entirely.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="app">
    <h2>The app on your computer</h2>
    <p>
      Phosphor Desktop and the Host run <a href="https://pi.dev">pi</a>, which talks to the model
      providers you set up. Those requests go straight from your computer to the provider, under
      the provider's own terms. Phosphor doesn't proxy, copy or log them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Desktop checks GitHub Releases for updates, so GitHub sees that request. Phosphor's servers
      don't.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="ping">
    <h2>The daily usage ping</h2>
    <p>
      Once a day, a few minutes after you open it, Phosphor Desktop tells this server that it is in
      use. This is the whole message:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{ping}</code></pre>
    <p>
      That is the version, the operating system, the processor type, and whether this is the
      install's first ping ever, this week or this month. Your computer works those three answers out
      from the dates of its own last ping, which it keeps and never sends. There is no install ID,
      account, machine name or anything about your work. The server adds the message to daily totals
      for that version and platform and keeps nothing else, so no stored row is about one install.
      It rate-limits with the same in-memory keyed hash as downloads, held for up to a day.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only official release builds send it, and only after a one-time notice in the app. Turn it off
      in <strong>Settings → Advanced → Privacy</strong>, which also shows exactly what the next ping
      would carry, or set <code>DO_NOT_TRACK=1</code> or <code>PHOSPHOR_NO_USAGE_PING=1</code> in
      the environment Phosphor starts in. The Host doesn't send it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="account">
    <h2>A Phosphor account</h2>
    <p>
      You only need an account to reach your own machines remotely. Signing in with GitHub creates
      it. The control server then keeps:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>your GitHub login, user id, name, avatar and email, as GitHub provides them;</li><li>
        each machine you connect: its label, hostname, platform, Phosphor version, public key, when
        it was last seen, and the number of lanes running (never what is in them);
      </li><li>the apps and browsers you've authorized, and when they were authorized;</li><li>
        a log of connecting, renaming and removing machines, kept for 180 days, without labels or
        addresses.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The request log notes your account id against each signed-in request. To rate-limit sign-in
      and API calls, the server holds your address in memory for up to an hour. It is never written
      to disk.
    </p>
    <p>
      The <a href="https://phosphor-app.saccolabs.com/">web app</a> keeps you signed in with one encrypted,
      first-party cookie. It is only used for signing in. The details are in the <a
        href={doc('remote-access.md#trust-boundary')}>remote access trust boundary</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="services">
    <h2>Services we rely on</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Cloudflare</strong> sits in front of both sites and sees your IP address, as any network
        provider does.
      </li><li>
        <strong>Supabase</strong> hosts the account database and sign-in. Its sign-in records can include
        your IP address and browser.
      </li><li>
        <strong>GitHub</strong> handles sign-in and hosts the downloads.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each one handles that data under its own privacy policy.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="delete">
    <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      Deleting your account removes it from Phosphor's database, along with your machines,
      authorized apps and their log. Any connected Host stops on its next check-in. Nothing on your
      own computers is touched. Download counts and usage pings aren't linked to anyone, so there's
      nothing in them to remove.
    </p>
    <p>
      Without an account, Phosphor's servers hold nothing about you. To remove the app, uninstall
      it. Its settings live on your computer.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="contact">
    <h2>Changes and questions</h2>
    <p>
      If this changes, this page changes in the same update, and the <a
        href={`${repo}/commits/main/apps/site/src/pages/privacy.astro`}>history of this page</a
      > is public. For questions, <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>open an issue</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
